WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.794 --> 00:00:09.710
BRIAN STEVENS: Funny
thing is that we gave Urs

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.130
20 minutes to talk about
all the innovation.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.707
You know, I sit
about 10 feet away.

00:00:13.707 --> 00:00:14.915
He used to be four feet away.

00:00:14.915 --> 00:00:16.890
He's moving a little bit
farther away from me.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.530
But he could actually talk
all day on just innovation.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.220
And trust me, you
wouldn't get bored.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.344
And he's the one who
sold me on coming here,

00:00:23.344 --> 00:00:25.760
because it was just that belief
of all the innovation that

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.384
was inside Google, and they were
going to point that innovation

00:00:28.384 --> 00:00:29.820
cannon, that cloud.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:30.820
How can you not win?

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.361
And in the process
of winning, you're

00:00:32.361 --> 00:00:34.550
redefining what the
industry thinks cloud is.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:35.680
So it's been fantastic.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.850
But on top of
innovation, Google's

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.120
really different in
another way as well,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.010
and GCP is, we're open, right?

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.176
And that's not by accident.

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:44.800
It's just that we
fundamentally believe

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.520
that the cloud that has the
best features and the best

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.490
capabilities, but that's
built on open technology

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:55.330
and open APIs, just presents the
most value to your end users.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:58.040
So that really is our mission.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.120
And because of that, a
lot of our innovation

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.994
is just happening
externally, because it just

00:01:01.994 --> 00:01:03.096
advances so much faster.

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:04.970
And when you try to do
things in proprietary,

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:06.803
all you're really doing
is creating friction

00:01:06.803 --> 00:01:11.500
to both adopt and impediments
for people to actually leave.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.280
So to us, we actually
really internalize us,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.550
and it really influences choices
that we make on a daily basis.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:22.190
So at GCP, we're really trying
not to reinvent the wheel.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.546
If a de facto API
already exists out there

00:01:24.546 --> 00:01:26.420
and we want to have a
similar service in GCP,

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.110
then we adopt that API.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.000
So the whole HBase
ecosystem and technology

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.947
was created because of the
Bigtable white paper by Google.

00:01:34.947 --> 00:01:37.030
And so when we actually
took that internal service

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.320
and said we want to host
that as a GCP service,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.800
instead of using
that internal API,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.960
we put the open
source HBase API on it

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.890
because it was just better for
end users to be able to adopt.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.770
And the second is if an
open source stack is already

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.500
out there and it has really
great adoption around it,

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.330
then you're going to want to
use it in the cloud as well.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:54.579
You shouldn't have to convert.

00:01:54.579 --> 00:01:57.364
So actually we've been bringing
those in as managed services.

00:01:57.364 --> 00:01:59.280
And when we bring them
in as managed services,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.550
there's all this
amazing thing we

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.385
can do in terms of
integrating them, making

00:02:02.385 --> 00:02:07.140
them more reliable, integrating
telemetry, more performant.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.688
So you'll actually
stay for the value

00:02:08.688 --> 00:02:09.979
and not because of the lock in.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:11.479
And that's some of
things that we're

00:02:11.479 --> 00:02:15.440
doing around Redis,
MySQL, Hadoop, and Spark.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.470
The third is in open
source, you can't draft.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.399
You really gotta get
involved in upstream.

00:02:20.399 --> 00:02:21.940
If you really care
about technologies

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.300
and you're building
a platform on it,

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.060
you can't just be
downstream and consume.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.184
You've got to get involved.

00:02:26.184 --> 00:02:29.220
And that's what we've been
doing for years around Linux,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.080
and now with Docker,
Chef, Puppet, Spinnaker,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.282
Terraform, and most
recently Cloud Foundry.

00:02:35.282 --> 00:02:36.740
And then the last
point around this

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.430
is that if there is technology
that doesn't exist out there

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.810
but we really feel like it
needs to be part of cloud,

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:42.800
you have a choice.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.235
Most people just differentiate
and build it internally.

00:02:45.235 --> 00:02:46.610
Instead we've
actually said we're

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.495
going to do that publicly
and do it openly.

00:02:48.495 --> 00:02:50.620
And that's what we've been
doing around TenserFlow.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.470
We've been doing it around
Kubernetes, Apache Beam,

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.860
first Apache project I think
that was created by Google,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:00.510
which we think is the future
of MapReduce, and GRPC.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.890
So we know the model works.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.560
Open innovation works.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:04.900
We've proven that.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.350
Two of the most significant
intellectual property

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:11.320
areas for Google are machine
learning and containers.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.210
And both of those,
we consciously

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.170
chose to develop them openly.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:19.910
And now because of that, we've
looked at both Kubernetes

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:20.940
and TensorFlow.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.190
And on GitHub, those are now
the number one and number

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.670
two most engaged projects
on all of GitHub.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.570
And we just believe
that innovation

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:31.230
doesn't have to be held
for proprietary advantages.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.000
Kubernetes and TensorFlow,
it's not a marketing stunt.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.750
They are both
better technologies

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:39.670
because of that community
involvement upstream.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.400
But it's not always about
some big massive open source

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.210
project.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:46.520
Even the smallest of things
can really get in the way

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.030
and impede adoption.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:50.710
And so last year right
after I came here,

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:54.200
I started talking to some
colleagues for the Wall Street

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.150
banks.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:58.640
And what they really said
is that cloud's not done.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.120
There's all these future
things that they actually need.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.210
And what they want to do is now
they knew Google was serious,

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.120
they wanted to start having
more open conversations

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.370
about the advancement of
the future requirements.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:10.210
And primarily these were things
around networking and security

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.377
and then how do you actually
deploy applications.

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:14.460
Because the reality is
what they didn't want to do

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.970
is have siloed conversation
with each of the top three cloud

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:18.339
providers, because
then we all go in

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:19.540
and implement those
things differently.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:20.700
We're all going to do them.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.350
And it just creates all this
gratuitous differentiation.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:25.100
And to be honest,
that's one of the things

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.539
that's plagued the whole entire
IT industry for many years.

00:04:28.539 --> 00:04:30.080
And so it's early
days in the effort.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.538
We actually created
working groups.

00:04:31.538 --> 00:04:34.217
And believe it or not,
Google, Azure, and AWS

00:04:34.217 --> 00:04:36.300
are all in the same room
with these top customers.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.925
And we're really trying to work
it out and have open dialogues.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.240
So the one nice thing about
our commitment to open

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.310
means we actually get
to work with partners

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:47.390
in a whole new way.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:48.330
It's much deeper.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.130
Often, that looks
like partners actually

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:53.710
just port their products
to GCP, and we're

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.230
answering help desk tickets.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.920
And when we really
engage around open

00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:59.140
and our partners
are open as well,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.140
it actually allows us to
work with them on code.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.094
So it's really been a lot more
fun and a lot more productive.

00:05:04.094 --> 00:05:05.760
So one of the companies
that recently we

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.267
actually formed this type of
relationship with is Pivotal.

00:05:08.267 --> 00:05:10.100
And so Pivotal doesn't
need an introduction,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:13.540
but they're the corporate
company behind Cloud Foundry.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.500
And Cloud Foundry is a
platform as a service.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:16.916
And they've done
a really good job

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:20.890
of helping people really convert
to agile methodology and cloud

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:21.390
native.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:23.660
And they're having a lot
of successful on-premise

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:24.900
and cloud.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.300
So Onsi Fakhouri is
here from Pivotal.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.280
He runs engineering.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.586
And I wanted to chat
with them a little bit

00:05:29.586 --> 00:05:30.502
about our partnership.

00:05:30.502 --> 00:05:32.615
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.670
We can stand, or set
the tone and stand.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:42.500
So I can't sit, you'll
give me massive shit.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:46.007
So how did the relationship with
Pivotal and GCP get started?

00:05:46.007 --> 00:05:46.840
ONSI FAKHOURI: Sure.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.589
So we had a mutual customer
come along and say,

00:05:49.589 --> 00:05:51.630
I really like Pivotal
Cloud Foundry and the value

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:52.338
that it gives me.

00:05:52.338 --> 00:05:54.620
And I'm really liking GCP.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.810
Can I get these two
things together please?

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:59.582
And so we started
working together,

00:05:59.582 --> 00:06:02.520
codeveloping on a
solution to really launch

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.800
PCF on top of GCP.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.180
And it's been just
really, really powerful.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.400
And now we're seeing
growing demand for it.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.734
So that initial seed has gone
on to bear a lot of fruit.

00:06:11.734 --> 00:06:14.066
BRIAN STEVENS: So tell me a
little bit about two things.

00:06:14.066 --> 00:06:16.330
One, so what's that
collaboration felt like working

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:17.300
with our engineers?

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.070
Two, what's the experience
of sort of new to GCP?

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:21.070
What has that felt that?

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.361
ONSI FAKHOURI: So, I mean,
it's really blown our minds,

00:06:23.361 --> 00:06:25.305
the level of collaboration.

00:06:25.305 --> 00:06:27.430
Jay Marshall, Eric Johnson,
Evan Brown, these folks

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:31.467
came in-- San Francisco, the
Pivotal office in San Francisco

00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:32.550
is just across the street.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.200
And we had these
folks from Google

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.300
show up, sit down
with us, pair with us,

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:40.660
working day in and day out to
really perfect the technology,

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:43.540
get it working, and
get it deployed.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:47.050
And it was just really
incredible collaboration.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:48.162
It sort of blew my mind.

00:06:48.162 --> 00:06:49.620
We had to make some
changes to PCF.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.620
We also had to make
some changes to GCP.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:53.840
And they made those changes.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:55.210
It was like, what?

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:58.050
It was really impressive.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.240
And then something
something crazy happened.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.430
So we deployed
PCF on top of GCP.

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.540
And then one of my
engineers came to me

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.760
and said-- he came up to
my office and was like,

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.129
"Onsi, can I give a talk?"

00:07:09.129 --> 00:07:10.670
I'm like, "Sure,
you can give a talk.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:11.650
What do you want to
give a talk about?"

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.753
And he said, "I want to
give a talk about GCP."

00:07:13.753 --> 00:07:15.690
I was like, "Huh?"

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.411
And you know what happened
there was an aha moment.

00:07:18.411 --> 00:07:20.160
So here's this engineer
who's been working

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.580
on other IaaSes for
a while, and he's

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.075
had this first
experience with GCP.

00:07:24.075 --> 00:07:27.700
And he goes, "This
is just so different.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:28.480
It's elegant.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:29.690
It's thoughtful.

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:30.570
It's performant.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.407
It's stable."

00:07:31.407 --> 00:07:33.490
Performant and stable are
really important for us.

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:36.619
We have CI pipelines the
drive IaaSes really hard,

00:07:36.619 --> 00:07:37.910
like bring them to their knees.

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:41.440
And so if they're flaky
or if they're not up,

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:43.380
my developers are
not productive.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:47.846
And we saw this opportunity
to jump onto a platform that

00:07:47.846 --> 00:07:48.720
was super productive.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.830
And then I started
to dig into it a bit.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:54.366
And you know, the value
per dollar, again, just

00:07:54.366 --> 00:07:54.990
off the charts.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:59.140
And so we saw an opportunity
to really sort of pivot over.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:02.330
And the nice thing
about PCF is that for us

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:04.640
and for our customers, you
know, we're cloud agnostic.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:05.635
You can run on AWS.

00:08:05.635 --> 00:08:06.510
You can on the Azure.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:07.420
You can on GCP.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:10.550
And so making that move,
it's just very easy.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.940
And so we're now
very eager customers.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:15.770
And we are,
internally at Pivotal,

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.700
going to be moving many
of our workloads, most

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.072
of our workloads over to GCP.

00:08:19.072 --> 00:08:21.530
And we're just really excited
for where this partnership is

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:22.390
going to go.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:23.690
BRIAN STEVENS: So what's next?

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.500
ONSI FAKHOURI: Well
lots is coming.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.232
I don't have anything
to share today,

00:08:27.232 --> 00:08:29.690
but in the next few weeks we
should have a lot of good news

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:30.500
to share with you all.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:31.458
BRIAN STEVENS: Awesome.

00:08:31.458 --> 00:08:34.905
So I will say that there's
a lot of assignments,

00:08:34.905 --> 00:08:36.280
there's a lot of
stuff that needs

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:38.113
to be done in engineering,
and not all of it

00:08:38.113 --> 00:08:39.364
is fund and glamorous.

00:08:39.364 --> 00:08:40.530
There's a lot of dirty work.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.669
And when we actually started
putting people on Pivotal

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:45.940
and working on Cloud
Foundry, I think

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.390
they're some of our happiest,
most engaged engineers,

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:49.270
and they love working
with your team.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:50.240
ONSI FAKHOURI:
It's just been fun.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:51.240
It's just been really fun.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:52.070
So that's been great.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:52.420
BRIAN STEVENS: Awesome.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:52.880
ONSI FAKHOURI: Awesome.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:53.380
Thank you.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:54.640
Have you a good one.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.920
BRIAN STEVENS: So the
transition to the cloud

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.737
for a born in the cloud
developer or a startup

00:09:04.737 --> 00:09:06.320
is actually pretty
simple because they

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.310
don't have to go through
a transition at all.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:11.530
But for most businesses,
you have a large legacy

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:15.147
of installed applications,
infrastructures, networking,

00:09:15.147 --> 00:09:16.855
administrative
methodologies, developers,

00:09:16.855 --> 00:09:18.689
and just institutional
knowledge.

00:09:18.689 --> 00:09:20.230
So one of the things
that we actually

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:23.301
think about at GCP a lot is
what's that path to adoption

00:09:23.301 --> 00:09:23.800
look like?

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:25.670
What can we actually
do so it doesn't

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.610
look like this separate
thing, and how do we actually

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:32.690
find what's creating the
most impedance and friction

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:33.800
and eliminate that?

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:35.560
So I want to talk a
little bit about that.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:36.780
And the story goes
back eight years ago,

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.738
because the first cloud
service that Google had

00:09:38.738 --> 00:09:40.251
was Google App Engine.

00:09:40.251 --> 00:09:42.250
And as Urs talked about,
it's an amazing service

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:45.029
because developers can
publish these services,

00:09:45.029 --> 00:09:47.070
and they don't have to
worry about anything else.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:48.430
They don't have to worry
about the reliability

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:49.600
because we take care of it.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:50.660
They don't have to
worry about scaling

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:51.730
up because we take care of it.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:53.605
Scaling down, we take
care of it, all the way

00:09:53.605 --> 00:09:54.500
back down to zero.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.805
They aren't hiring
database administrators.

00:09:56.805 --> 00:09:59.430
And they aren't dealing with any
of the other underlying issues

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.080
and managing OS upgrades.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.820
It's a really powerful platform.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.810
And the end result is you get
this amazing service at scale,

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.560
and your operational
burden is near zero.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:10.910
But for some, that jump to
PaaS is just a little bit

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:12.076
of a bridge too far.

00:10:12.076 --> 00:10:13.450
And sometimes the
easiest pattern

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:16.090
for the first part
of cloud adoption

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.080
is just to have an
API around a VM.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:20.620
And that really
makes it much more

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.536
easier to consume because
you're already doing

00:10:22.536 --> 00:10:24.350
that inside your data centers.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.200
And that was really
the mindset on why,

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.320
even though we had a PaaS
is why we invested in IaaS

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.860
and why GCE came
out three years ago.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:35.810
And obviously now
that seems right,

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:37.527
because internally
when we watch and we

00:10:37.527 --> 00:10:39.860
look at the growth of our
platforms, when we measure it,

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.010
GCE is one of those
services that we actually

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:44.230
measure with log scale
just to watch the growth.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:47.416
So I think obviously it's really
helping enterprises a lot.

00:10:47.416 --> 00:10:49.540
But we don't actually see
this as in, you see a lot

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.000
in the world like, it's a silo.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.501
It's a platform as
a service choice,

00:10:52.501 --> 00:10:54.416
or it's an infrastructure
as a service choice.

00:10:54.416 --> 00:10:55.890
It's really not that way at all.

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:59.179
What we're finding is people are
coming to us initially for GCE,

00:10:59.179 --> 00:11:00.720
and what then they're
doing over time

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:04.200
is they're adopting service
by service for zero ops.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:08.080
You might start on VMs, and then
you start to use a managed load

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:11.340
balancer, or you're
using a managed firewall,

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:13.290
or then you start to
use our auto scaler,

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:16.740
or then you start to use our
PaaS for any type of database.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.490
And so you never
really made this choice

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:19.580
of what silo I'm in.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:22.490
You really just found the
easiest pattern to adopt,

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:25.150
and then you actually grow into
more of a zero ops environment

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:26.480
as you go.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.850
And the story's kind of
similar for networking.

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:31.390
When you think of
how cloud originated,

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:34.090
it really was just a
separate subnet in the cloud,

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:36.025
all with public facing IPs.

00:11:36.025 --> 00:11:38.290
And now with GCP, we
recognize that's not

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.510
the right model for enterprise.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:42.940
So what we've done is
now when you use GCP,

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.410
it looks much more like private
subnets in the cloud, all

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:46.295
with private IPs.

00:11:46.295 --> 00:11:48.320
You can easily bridge
those together.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.349
You can easily put a
firewall in front of them.

00:11:50.349 --> 00:11:52.140
And then most importantly,
you can actually

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:54.957
bridge that network right
into your on-premise network

00:11:54.957 --> 00:11:56.790
without transiting over
the public internet.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:58.335
So it feels far
more like cloud's

00:11:58.335 --> 00:12:02.620
evolved to feel far more like
it's a data center extension.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.650
On the container side,
we talk a lot about that

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.790
because that's
just our language.

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:08.444
Google grew up on containers.

00:12:08.444 --> 00:12:10.110
Developers inside of
Google don't really

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:11.234
know about infrastructure.

00:12:11.234 --> 00:12:12.900
They wouldn't be
productive if they did.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:16.620
They're just publishing their
code in services, on the board,

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:18.890
and they get everything
else for free.

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:20.600
And that's why Google
invested so much

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:23.480
in cgroups over a decade
ago, because cgroups

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.190
was that technology inside
of Linux that allowed

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:28.050
that isolation to happen.

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.864
So you get multiple containers
on a single Linux image.

00:12:30.864 --> 00:12:32.780
And it doesn't provide
the security isolation,

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:35.502
but it provides the
needed resource isolation.

00:12:35.502 --> 00:12:36.710
And that was the first phase.

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:38.910
And now with Docker,
the container movements

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:42.370
are light, because on top of
that Linux kernel isolation,

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:44.770
now what you're getting
is application confinement

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:46.810
and application portability.

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:48.300
So it's really powerful.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.790
And so you actually can
Dockerize applications,

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:52.540
containerize applications
and deliver them

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:55.150
across multiple platforms,
multiple OSes, multiple clouds.

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:56.336
That's really liberating.

00:12:56.336 --> 00:12:57.835
It's really, if I
had to compare it,

00:12:57.835 --> 00:12:59.500
it's really what
VMware and Linux

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:02.960
did long ago by normalizing
onto our hardware platforms.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:05.340
And now Docker's bringing
that to a whole new level.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:07.940
And so you have to think
that five years time,

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:12.370
10 years time, transcoding
VMs as a method of migration

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:16.730
or portability from one VM
format to another has to end.

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:19.790
So this is really great
for the IT industry.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:22.520
But what it needed was a
management and orchestration

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:23.270
system.

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:26.540
So think about how you would
manage ASX in your environment

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:30.290
today if you didn't have
either vSphere or vCenter.

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:33.210
So because of that, contributing
Kubernetes to open source

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:35.940
was just the obvious
choice for Google,

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:39.210
because if we don't, it'll
hold back the ubiquity of what

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:41.020
this container movement can be.

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:45.510
And then second, all these other
different bespoke solutions

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:47.280
for managing
containers will arise.

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:49.580
And so you won't really get
sort of the standardization

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:52.340
that you want around
management and orchestration.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:55.000
And so kind of calling
it done right now.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.610
Like Kubernetes
has really become

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.490
that de facto management
system for Docker.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:02.460
And you see that on GitHub as
the number one most engaged

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:03.170
project.

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:04.520
TensorFlow was second.

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:08.040
Third was actually Docker--
really powerful trio.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.540
And so this week actually
marks-- it was on Monday,

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:13.880
the release of the open source
Kubernetes 1.4 milestone.

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:18.981
And hoping for a great GKE
launch of the same tomorrow

00:14:18.981 --> 00:14:19.480
actually.

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:23.319
And the one nice thing that
1.4 brought was federation.

00:14:23.319 --> 00:14:24.860
We've been working
on it for a while.

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.120
Pieces have been coming
out in the prior releases.

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:29.370
And what that allows you to
do is now with containers,

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.520
it allows you to have
multiple clusters

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.459
of containers all managed
with a single control plane.

00:14:34.459 --> 00:14:36.750
And it's a really powerful
thing because these clusters

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:38.460
can be on-premise.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:39.830
They can be in the cloud.

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:42.475
And in GCP, they can be
multiple zones in the cloud.

00:14:42.475 --> 00:14:43.850
So what you get
out of that is it

00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:45.475
makes it really simple
for a developer.

00:14:45.475 --> 00:14:47.230
A developer, with
one command, can

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.170
publish a service, a container
based service, that's

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:52.380
multihomed across
multiple clusters,

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:55.500
sitting behind a single
static IP address.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.240
And then our global
load balancer

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:01.710
can route to the right
cluster based on latency,

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.260
based on capacity.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:06.660
And so you get this really
powerful low latency, high

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:11.570
performance solution without
any extra operational costs.

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:13.480
And this was a big thing.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:16.650
I didn't figure out what
Pokemon was on Friday night

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:19.910
when Sunder made the
call that there basically

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:21.989
our QPS was spiking.

00:15:21.989 --> 00:15:23.530
And one of the tools
that we actually

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:26.350
used to help that
out was actually they

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:28.810
were one of the earliest
consumers of the federation

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:31.716
capability, meaning
multiple zones of GKE.

00:15:31.716 --> 00:15:33.840
And you're going to
hear the whole story,

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.090
I think this afternoon.

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:41.230
So I'm really proud that open
source and Linux is a trusted

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:42.230
enterprise platform.

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:45.100
You know, I spent almost
13 years at Red Hat.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:46.260
It was my personal mission.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:48.759
And it was just because I so
believed in that Unix paradigm,

00:15:48.759 --> 00:15:52.460
which became the Linux
paradigm, and that Intel

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:56.130
like commoditzing underlying
hardware underneath made sense,

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:58.600
and that it had to be open.

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:00.479
But the on ramp to
cloud is bigger.

00:16:00.479 --> 00:16:02.270
It's a heterogeneous
environment out there,

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:03.700
and it includes more than Linux.

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:04.660
It includes Windows.

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:09.730
So Windows has really been a top
priority for GCP for some time.

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:12.100
We now have Windows Server 2000.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.830
We have 2012.

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:17.680
2016 I think is
coming in October.

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:19.700
We have a full .NET stack.

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:22.460
We have Visual Studio
for developers.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.270
We have PowerShell support.

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:26.100
We have SQL Server images.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:29.960
We have license portability
that Microsoft supports now.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:34.910
And we build .NET clients across
all of the GCP APIs to make it

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:36.360
easier for developers.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:39.730
So we really think that
this marriage of Windows

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:42.180
with all the amazing
things inside of GCP,

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:46.420
like fast bootable custom VM
shapes in the fast network,

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:48.600
it really all of a
sudden means that GCP

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:52.340
makes this awesome
platform for Windows shops.

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:53.350
So more on friction.

00:16:53.350 --> 00:16:57.620
The first adopters of cloud
naturally are AWS users.

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:00.030
And what we found though
is that a lot of AWS users

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:02.085
come to GCP because
of the differentiation

00:17:02.085 --> 00:17:03.585
that Urs was talking
about earlier--

00:17:03.585 --> 00:17:05.940
you know, these faster VMs.

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:08.470
We have these shapes that you
don't have to be stuck to,

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:12.380
a set of specified core
sizes and specified VM sizes.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:14.819
You can actually pick
an arbitrary shape.

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:17.319
Our sustained use discounts,
because people, believe or not,

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:18.760
don't love RIs.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:20.790
Our private global
network-- you know,

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:23.710
no need to schedule reboots
because of live migration,

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:26.069
and the BigQuery analytics
that we talked about today

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:28.920
that makes Redshift kind of look
like a departmental warehouse

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.014
that you have to
manage yourself.

00:17:31.014 --> 00:17:33.430
And so a few years back when
we were saying we really need

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:36.170
to bring monitoring
capability to GCP,

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:38.410
the acquisition we made
was a company, Stackdriver,

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:41.489
that was running on AWS&gt; And
one of the choices that most

00:17:41.489 --> 00:17:44.030
companies would make is say I'm
going to move that completely

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:47.189
to GCP and then use that for
a point of differentiation.

00:17:47.189 --> 00:17:49.230
Instead we said, we've
got enough differentiation

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:50.924
for when users are
actually using GCP.

00:17:50.924 --> 00:17:52.840
But if it's going to be
this multicloud world,

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:54.500
isn't a better
service for our users

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:57.200
if they're going to
be using AWS as well

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:59.707
that they're going to have a
single monitoring, alerting,

00:17:59.707 --> 00:18:01.540
and logging backplane
that works across all?

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:04.092
And so it's a bigger
investment on our part,

00:18:04.092 --> 00:18:05.550
but that's what
we're committed to.

00:18:08.152 --> 00:18:09.860
And so I love this
Uber quote, I mean one

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:12.100
because just it's a great
quote about Stackdriver.

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:17.221
But even more importantly
to that, what it signifies

00:18:17.221 --> 00:18:18.762
is that even in the
cloud world where

00:18:18.762 --> 00:18:21.137
we're doing so much of the
managing of the infrastructure

00:18:21.137 --> 00:18:23.180
and the managing
of the services,

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:24.949
end users still want telemetry.

00:18:24.949 --> 00:18:26.990
It's not like, make it
all work and don't tell me

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:27.800
how you did it.

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:30.133
And so that's what we're
trying to give with Stackdriver

00:18:30.133 --> 00:18:33.316
is, even as we're
orchestrating services,

00:18:33.316 --> 00:18:35.190
even when, if you've
picked a solution that's

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:37.470
patching your OS, you
still need to know that.

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:39.720
And so a big part of Stackdriver
is how do we actually

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:42.140
expose all of the telemetry
that's happening inside

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:47.760
of GCP so that it makes sure
that you have full awareness?

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:51.680
So cloud has really made
infrastructure programmable.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:53.230
You don't file a
help desk ticket

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:54.600
to get a server any longer.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:57.050
You use an API.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:00.030
We've now made everything
programmatic-- firewalls,

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:02.670
virtual machines, networks,
you know, the network,

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:05.140
any data store you want.

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:08.120
So it's quickly becoming
a foregone conclusion

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:10.500
that infrastructure
is now programmable.

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:13.360
But to really realize the
dream, the next phase,

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:16.780
that has to extend into
applications and services.

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:19.220
And we're making progress.

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:21.930
Developers now packaging their
applications under containers

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:24.820
is super helpful for that,
because now they're lightweight

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:26.986
and they're portable so
they can be automated.

00:19:26.986 --> 00:19:28.360
And then you bring
in Kubernetes,

00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:31.180
so now you can manage that
whole container environment

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:33.231
without managing the
underlying infrastructure.

00:19:33.231 --> 00:19:35.230
So the third phase that
I'd say we're kind of in

00:19:35.230 --> 00:19:37.229
and we haven't really
figured out as an industry

00:19:37.229 --> 00:19:39.370
yet is really how
do you actually

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:41.490
represent microservices
and services,

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:43.490
and then how do you
actually wire those together

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:45.810
and deploy those as an entity?

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:47.750
So there's a lot of
choices on how to do that.

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:48.411
We have some.

00:19:48.411 --> 00:19:49.410
There's a lot out there.

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:51.560
But the industry hasn't
really consolidated

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:53.610
on what that de facto
model for that is.

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:55.420
And so I think that's
the next phase.

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.430
And unless you unlock
that, then you really

00:19:57.430 --> 00:19:59.050
hold back a lot of
the standardization

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:01.990
and we move on to
solving the next problem.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:05.280
So at GCP, obviously we
have UIs for everything.

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:07.540
But we're putting
everything behind an API.

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:09.600
So we're putting
infrastructure behind an API.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:13.260
We're putting all the services
behind an API, microservices,

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.610
and application stacks.

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:18.060
And so we really think
that Cloud and GCP

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.059
are enabling the next generation
of what an ecosystem is

00:20:21.059 --> 00:20:21.850
going to look like.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:23.350
And so the reason
I think about that

00:20:23.350 --> 00:20:29.150
is the days of the selling of
binaries and software stacks

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:31.990
as a commercial
ecosystem-- you know,

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:33.852
it leaves a lot to be desired.

00:20:33.852 --> 00:20:35.560
Nobody likes receiving
stacks of software

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:37.460
that they have to configure,
et cetera et cetera.

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:39.880
But what people really want
is what happens after that.

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:41.810
How do we actually start
to consume capability

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:44.274
through a service
and through an API?

00:20:44.274 --> 00:20:45.940
And so we really think
that's the future

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:47.106
of the commercial ecosystem.

00:20:47.106 --> 00:20:49.857
And we're really at the very
beginning part of that journey.

00:20:49.857 --> 00:20:52.440
It's like, how do we get out of
delivering stacks of software,

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:53.540
even in the cloud,
because they're still

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:55.610
kind of doing that as
well with the ecosystem.

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:57.235
And instead what
you're doing is you're

00:20:57.235 --> 00:20:58.920
provisioning
capability from the API

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:01.230
and allow people to consume it.

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:04.314
And that's a big reason
why we acquired Apigee.

00:21:04.314 --> 00:21:05.730
Because what we
want to do is make

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:08.700
it easier for businesses
to take their services

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:12.462
and enter that into
that API economy.

00:21:12.462 --> 00:21:14.837
At the cloud providers,
from an API perspective,

00:21:14.837 --> 00:21:15.670
we're already there.

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:17.980
But there's so much value
that businesses can have

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:20.930
if they can bring their services
into this new ecosystem.

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:24.370
And Apigee is so far ahead in
figuring out a lot of this.

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:27.760
Their API management
deals with all aspects

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:29.834
of authorization and security.

00:21:29.834 --> 00:21:30.625
It deals with DDoS.

00:21:30.625 --> 00:21:31.712
It deals with integration.

00:21:31.712 --> 00:21:32.545
It deals with quota.

00:21:32.545 --> 00:21:33.840
It deals with billing.

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:35.970
It deals with specifying an SLA.

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:37.790
And those are all the
facilities that you

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:40.750
need if you really want to enter
this business to business API

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:42.770
economy.

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:45.920
And so GCP users are actually
going to be able to now,

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:48.242
when there's Apigee
powered services,

00:21:48.242 --> 00:21:50.700
they can actually integrate
them whether those services are

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:52.491
running on another
cloud or whether they're

00:21:52.491 --> 00:21:53.560
running on-premise.

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:56.837
And then likewise builders that
are actually building on GCP

00:21:56.837 --> 00:21:59.170
are going to be able to use
that whole Apigee framework,

00:21:59.170 --> 00:22:01.086
and they're going to
easily be able to publish

00:22:01.086 --> 00:22:03.460
their services through
the Apigee APIs

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:05.880
to meet whole new markets.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:09.479
So as you might
expect, Apigee and GCP

00:22:09.479 --> 00:22:10.770
have a lot of common customers.

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:12.599
It's not really that surprising.

00:22:12.599 --> 00:22:15.140
So I actually want to introduce
you to one of those customers

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:15.640
today.

00:22:15.640 --> 00:22:18.474
So please welcome Naveen
Krishan of the Home Depot.

